WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.425
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.817 --> 00:00:08.900
DAVE STANKE: All right,
so my name is Dave Stanke.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.090
I'm part of the Developer
Relations team here at Google.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.212
And with me is
Christopher Sanson.

00:00:14.212 --> 00:00:15.920
He's the product
manager for Cloud Build.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.360
He'll be up a little later.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.310
And that character
up in the top right,

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.200
that's Billy the
Builder Bee, and she'll

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.670
be helping us out later on.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.190
So we're shifting left.

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.998
What does that mean?

00:00:27.998 --> 00:00:31.970
It means to incorporate critical
processes early in the software

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.285
development cycle.

00:00:33.285 --> 00:00:35.480
What does that mean?

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.150
Well, we can usually model a
software development process

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.090
as a left to right
flow, where we

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.700
go from code, through
some transformations,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.940
see if it's any good,
ship it, and then operate,

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.000
monitor on it, as it's live.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.780
And we model this as
a left to right flow.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.830
Shifting left means moving the
important steps of validation

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.110
and quality control
earlier in the process,

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:02.192
closer to the left.

00:01:02.192 --> 00:01:03.650
But of course,
software development

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.590
doesn't happen in
a single stream.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.480
We usually have
lots of these flows.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.150
And in a traditional process,
every developer or team

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.960
progresses independently.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.140
They're focused on writing
more and more code,

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.530
until at the last minute,
everything collides.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.630
And all at once,
all of these bits

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.230
are meeting for the first time.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.350
And we're hoping for a quick,
thumbs up, let's ship it.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.930
But when you have a
whole bunch of code,

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.580
it's strangers to each other.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.850
And you shove them
all in a room at once,

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:33.630
they might not get along.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.970
And so it's more
likely that we're

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.220
going to have merge conflicts.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.178
We're going to have
terrible security holes.

00:01:40.178 --> 00:01:41.720
We're going to have
incompatibilities

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.130
between some systems.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.240
And this is why in a traditional
waterfall-style development

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.660
process, you often have
huge, lengthy merge times.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.970
You often have long lead times.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.560
And you often have
terrible weeks

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.260
before and after
a major release.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.080
So we shift left.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.240
We move processes earlier
to get earlier feedback

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.588
on small checks
all along the way.

00:02:04.588 --> 00:02:07.130
So if you take two small pieces
and you validate each of them

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.139
independently, and
then when they combine,

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.180
the only thing we
actually have to test then

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:16.260
is what arises between the
connections between them.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.250
So what are we shifting left?

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.840
Well, you can shift
left lots of things.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.080
First off, you can
shift your merges left.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.670
And this is
continuous integration

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:27.350
in the original meaning--

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.600
that instead of having
long-lived branches

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.930
that hang out on a developer's
machine for a long time,

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.710
we're going to merge
at least once a day,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:35.810
preferably more often.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.310
And each one of
those merges is then

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.590
small, relatively pain-free.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:44.180
And we don't have "the
merge" that everyone dreads.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.160
We can also shift
left on security.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.730
So a lot of times,
traditionally, we

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.650
put together a big
software release.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.420
And then just before we ship,
we go to the security people

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.290
and we're like,
it's good, right?

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.340
Give us a thumbs up.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.330
And they say, slow down.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:01.010
Well, we can do a lot better
if we do security validation

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.680
earlier and oftener.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.210
So within code,
you can do things

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.010
like automated fuzz
testing to make sure

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:10.370
that your code actually
has some protection.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.590
You can test
individual components,

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.570
look at open source
packages you might

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.580
be using, using
vulnerability analysis,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:22.610
and generally have affirmation
that your code is pretty secure

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:24.083
all along the way.

00:03:24.083 --> 00:03:26.000
And at the end, you're
only validating, again,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.580
that very outer
perimeter of the code.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.248
And then you can
shift left on testing.

00:03:31.248 --> 00:03:33.290
And this is what we're
going to really dig into--

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.330
testing early and often.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.010
And why do we want
to test early?

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:38.973
To catch bugs early.

00:03:38.973 --> 00:03:40.640
I'd bet a lot of folks
here are familiar

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.160
with the research that's
been done by NIST and IBM

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.490
talking about how finding
a bug in development

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:51.107
costs way less time and energy
than finding one in production.

00:03:51.107 --> 00:03:52.940
The numbers I've heard
are anywhere from 6x,

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:55.640
to 30x, to 100x.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.650
But certainly, you'd
rather catch the bug early.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:03:59.150
Why?

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:00.350
Why is it so much cheaper?

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:02.100
Well, I think there's
a couple of reasons.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.100
One, if you catch it
early, it hasn't had time

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.050
to have downstream effects.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.750
The other system components
aren't aware of that bug.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.780
They're not working around
that bug, as they sometimes do.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.450
And two, if you catch a bug
while a developer's still

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:16.640
working on that process,
it's still in their mind.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.470
There's still in that
flow, and so they

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.950
don't have to rehydrate
and reconstruct,

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.550
what was I thinking when I wrote
that code with the bug in it?

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.175
So for these reasons,
it's really great

00:04:25.175 --> 00:04:29.630
to fix our bugs early
and be testing early.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.840
Oh, and one quick
bit of science here.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.397
From the State of
DevOps Report--

00:04:34.397 --> 00:04:35.980
and actually, I
believe there's a card

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.820
on every one of your
seats encouraging

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.860
you to participate-- the
State of DevOps Report

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.560
does analysis finding
out what practices

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.380
are done by teams that have
the most software quality.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:48.590
And they find that continuous
testing predicts continuous

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:52.010
delivery, which predicts
delivery performance--

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:54.980
how often we ship, and how
fast we get from code to live--

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:57.860
and availability,
how much of the time

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.860
our services are available.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.800
All right, so let's look at
testing and how we can shift

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:04.740
left on it.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.680
First of all, some definitions.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.430
So we often talk about testing
as the testing hierarchy.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:11.140
We start at the bottom
with unit tests.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:14.140
And unit tests, of course,
are the foundation.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.720
Unit tests are where we
test small functionality

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.820
of our code, kind of within
the frameworks of our code.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:22.660
And honestly, these are
a mostly solved problem--

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:23.500
maybe.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.550
But most modern frameworks
have unit testing built in.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.110
It generally can be run
pretty quickly as part of your

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:31.480
build and CI process.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.390
Integration testing is when
we test multiple components

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:38.500
together and when we make sure
that, when these things are

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.840
actually running, like
in production, that when

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.653
they work together, they don't
all of a sudden fall apart.

00:05:43.653 --> 00:05:44.320
This one worked.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:45.020
That one worked.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.220
But the application doesn't work
until you have both of them.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.480
And on top that we've
end-to-end testing,

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.960
which means usually running
a multi-step process

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.110
against a composed application.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.000
There's kind of a fuzzy line
between integration testing

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.470
and end-to-end testing.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:02.520
And the terms are sometimes
used interchangeably.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.020
Now, this diagram might look
like a tall, intimidating icy

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.580
mountain, but we're
going to show you

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.533
that it's not that hard to scale
to the pinnacle of testing.

00:06:09.533 --> 00:06:11.200
Today's focus is on
integration testing,

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.700
but everything we're looking
at can be used for E2E testing,

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:15.500
as well.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.980
So our goals for
integration testing--

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:20.460
we want high fidelity, high
isolation, high scalability,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.990
appropriate ephemerality.

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:25.742
And we always like
speed and low cost.

00:06:25.742 --> 00:06:27.200
We're not going to
get all the way,

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.500
but these are an
aspiration to shoot for.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:32.740
I'm going to show you some
ways that we can get close.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:35.080
So first of all, fidelity.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:36.580
Now, when we do
integration testing,

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:39.340
we start by creating
a system under test.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.220
That's the environment that
we're going to test against.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:45.280
We're not going to test
against prod, right?

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.020
And so we create a
simulation, effectively.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.270
And our goal here is fidelity.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:53.470
How similar is that system under
test to the production system?

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:55.000
The closer we get,
the more assured

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.990
we are that we're going to have
tests that reflect what reality

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.490
is going to be like, what our
users are going to experience.

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:05.470
And we're generally not
going be able to create

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:08.750
a full production
environment for each test,

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:10.425
although we'll
challenge that later on.

00:07:10.425 --> 00:07:12.550
So we're going to run
against a scaled-down version

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:16.390
of that full ecosystem
and aim to make it

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:19.280
as predictive as possible.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.930
Isolation-- this means that
each test is running completely

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:24.135
separate from the others.

00:07:24.135 --> 00:07:26.260
They're not going to stomp
on each other to compete

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:30.700
for processes, CPU, memory,
disk space, whatever.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.540
And they also don't connect to
the same external resources.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.330
If you have a shared
database or a prod API,

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:38.980
even if it's read-only,
it's entirely

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:41.560
possible for a runaway
read to block everyone else

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:42.760
from using it.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:45.092
Isolation also has
to do with security,

00:07:45.092 --> 00:07:47.050
so we want to configure
all of the resources we

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:50.063
use within tests to not
have public visibility.

00:07:50.063 --> 00:07:52.480
The last thing we want is for
someone to corrupt our tests

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:58.150
or somehow give us bad data that
then sneaks into prod somehow,

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:01.940
or run a bitcoin miner on
our testing infrastructure.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.480
And then scalability--
of course, we want big.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.100
How many tests can
we run at a time?

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:10.060
Ideally, every developer can
kick off all of their tests

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:11.090
at the same time.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:14.560
We don't want developers waiting
while their tests are queued up

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.120
behind their colleagues.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:17.530
Remember that flow we discussed?

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:19.335
Keep the developers going.

00:08:19.335 --> 00:08:21.460
And this means we want to
be able to scale the test

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:25.060
infrastructure elastically and
parallelize all of our tests.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:28.760
So that, of course, is a great
fit for cloud infrastructure.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:31.870
And then ephemerality,
which means,

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:33.640
how long does that
test infrastructure

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.870
hang around after we're
done doing our tests?

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.937
Ideally, it lasts just
until the test is over.

00:08:40.937 --> 00:08:42.020
And then it's gone, right?

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:43.720
We don't want to pay for it.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:47.920
And then we optimize the
ephemeral nature of the test.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.450
But the thing is, if the test
gets torn down immediately,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:55.180
then we don't have access
to go debug what happened

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:56.763
inside that test environment.

00:08:56.763 --> 00:08:58.180
Now of course,
best practices, you

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:00.850
should not be SSH-ing
into your prod machines.

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:03.730
But for a test machine, you can
learn a lot from a failed test

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:05.290
by going in and saying,
well, what's going on?

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:06.207
What process is there?

00:09:06.207 --> 00:09:09.160
What actual data got
written into that file?

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:11.360
Oh, it's not what I expected.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.120
So the best of
ephemerality means

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.200
that the system lasts
exactly as long as I need it

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:20.150
and not any longer.

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:22.060
And of course, speed is good.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:23.350
We like fast.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:24.910
This is important
both so that we

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:26.500
can keep our
developers productive

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:29.650
and also so we can increase
our mean time to deploy--

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:31.320
decrease, rather.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:33.520
Fast is better than slow.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:37.390
And finally, affordability--
developer time is expensive.

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:40.510
And generally, it's a good use
of money to give infrastructure

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:42.153
to your developers
for their tests,

00:09:42.153 --> 00:09:43.820
but it's always going
to be a trade-off.

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:46.195
And you certainly don't want
to pay more for your testing

00:09:46.195 --> 00:09:52.080
than it's worth for the sake
of your production environment.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.350
All right, now I'm going
to kick off a quick demo.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:09:58.840
There we go.

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:00.490
All right, so what
I'm going to do here

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:03.430
is I'm going to start off
a series of four tests.

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:05.260
And I'm not going to
explain them just yet,

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:06.635
I'm just going to
get them going.

00:10:06.635 --> 00:10:08.650
But I'm in my
editor here, and I'm

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:11.380
running this as a
GCloud submit command.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:14.320
Of course, you can also run
this from a trigger, as well.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:17.110
So we'll kick that off.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:20.650
And we will head to our UI,
and we'll be able to see.

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:23.150
This is the Cloud
console, and we're

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:24.400
going to look at the progress.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:25.942
And we're just
getting started on it.

00:10:28.458 --> 00:10:29.750
And back to the slides, please.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.630
And now, Christopher's
going to come up, talk

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:37.700
to us a little about what
testing is really like

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.260
and how close we
get to those goals.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:41.510
CHRISTOPHER SANSON: All right.

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:43.316
Thanks, Dave.

00:10:43.316 --> 00:10:44.465
Hey, everyone.

00:10:44.465 --> 00:10:46.340
So as Dave mentioned,
my name is Christopher.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:50.060
I'm a product manager at Google
working on Cloud Build, which

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:53.160
gives you a bit of a hint of
what I might be talking about.

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.390
But before we get into it, one
thing we hear from customers

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:59.100
all the time is, you come
to an event like this,

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.400
you read blogs, you read
up on the best practices,

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:02.660
and it's really inspiring.

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:06.100
You hear about things like shift
left, things like DevOps, SRE,

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:08.000
infrastructure as code.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.780
But when we talk to customers
in sort of back and reality

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:14.420
is that, look, this
is great, but this

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:15.770
is kind of like flying cars.

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:18.710
Like we get it, but it's so
far from where we are today

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:20.840
and our day-to-day reality.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.060
We need help both with
tooling and operations

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.370
and processes to help us
get to where we want to be.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:29.600
So I want to take a quick minute
to look at some test patterns

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:32.270
and test strategies that
we see in the real world

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:37.340
and what's happening when
things like shift left, I think,

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:40.580
are reasonably accepted
as best practices.

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:46.600
So I think the first test
that anyone ever runs

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:48.040
is the build itself.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.820
Every application starts
with compiling code, running

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:51.700
a build.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.550
And you iterate, and you test
your code, and you hack on it,

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:55.870
and you debug.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:58.090
And then eventually,
finally, the build is green.

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:00.910
And so that's, I
think, often as far

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:03.280
as a lot of different
applications ever go.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:06.557
If the build is green,
my app is green, right?

00:12:06.557 --> 00:12:08.140
But obviously in
modern architectures,

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:11.110
with things like microservices
and external dependencies,

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:12.880
just because your
build is green really

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.130
gives you no indication that
your ultimate application

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:17.117
is going to run correctly.

00:12:17.117 --> 00:12:18.700
There's a lot of
external dependencies

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:20.500
that can change out
from underneath you

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:21.520
while you're working.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.080
Other APIs can shift.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:26.020
And so you really need to
actually test and validate

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:28.090
that the way that
your application works

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:32.020
isn't just complete in and of
itself, but actually in the way

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:34.758
that it talks to other services.

00:12:34.758 --> 00:12:36.550
So the next thing we
see a lot of people do

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:38.770
is, OK, the build
is green, and they

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:40.300
start adding quality gates.

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:42.340
So these are static
analysis tools,

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:45.610
either commercial, like
Codecov or Coverty,

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:47.962
as well as open source things,
like JSHint and JSLint,

00:12:47.962 --> 00:12:48.670
things like that.

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:50.170
And quality gates
are really great.

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:51.710
They are very fast.

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:53.500
They're very cheap,
and they give you

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:57.220
a really good indication
of code quality.

00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:00.040
They could do everything from
validating against your style

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:03.340
guide, linting, giving you a
sense of how much code coverage

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:04.690
you have.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:05.800
The challenge though, is--

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:08.300
for anyone that's looked ahead,
if you look at the unit test

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:10.780
that we wrote, that
unit test isn't really

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:11.988
going to help you very much.

00:13:11.988 --> 00:13:14.530
All it's really going to do is
just get your code coverage up

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:18.430
to 80% so you can be allowed
to like merge your commit.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:20.410
So what we find
is, quality gates

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:23.662
are really helpful and really
powerful for indicating

00:13:23.662 --> 00:13:25.370
that your code is
probably going to work,

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:26.828
but it doesn't
actually do anything

00:13:26.828 --> 00:13:29.940
to verify that your
code will work.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.130
So after you've
had quality gates--

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.920
this is my personal favorite
test strategy and one

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:36.313
I've often employed.

00:13:36.313 --> 00:13:37.980
I like to call them
Humpty-Dumpty tests.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:39.980
This is where you don't
actually write any tests

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:42.030
till after the failure occurs.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:44.330
Something will go down in
prod-- you'll have an outage.

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:47.490
Your users will talk to you
and say, hey, this is down.

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:49.740
You figure out the recalls,
you submit a quick fix,

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:52.270
and then you go back
and write test coverage.

00:13:52.270 --> 00:13:54.660
Obviously, this is a big
reality of software development.

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:56.440
You're not going to
catch everything.

00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:58.482
But I think it's sort of
obvious you don't really

00:13:58.482 --> 00:14:00.780
want your users to be your QA.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:03.000
You want to test and
capture these things

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:04.903
before they reach users.

00:14:04.903 --> 00:14:06.570
By the way, I wanted
to give a shout-out

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:08.670
to our Illustrator, Tracy.

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:10.710
This is maybe my
favorite illustration

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.720
that I've ever had
at a talk I've given.

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:16.080
We were like, well, the
idea is, it's Humpty Dumpty.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:16.620
It breaks.

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:22.910
And this is what she came up
with, so shout-out to Tracy.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:27.410
And the last thing is classic,
almost cliched at this point.

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:29.600
It's sort of, well, it
worked on my machine.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.070
So we ran a survey on Twitter.

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:34.430
We asked people in
microservice architectures,

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:36.680
where's the first point in
time that you actually test

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:39.410
your app, versus the other
services it interacts with?

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:42.000
And this number was actually
a lot higher than we thought.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.270
So 43% of developers
were actually

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:48.750
testing it in their local
machine before pushing to prod.

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:51.590
So this is actually
encouraging, but this often

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:53.930
leads to the kind of,
it worked on my machine,

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:55.880
the classic failure
mode where you throw it

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:57.950
over the wall to the
deployment or ops team.

00:14:57.950 --> 00:14:59.450
And then there's
this back and forth

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:01.640
of blame game of like,
well, it worked for me,

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:04.130
but it's not working
for me, et cetera.

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:07.012
This happens because
they're different versions,

00:15:07.012 --> 00:15:08.720
there's different
OSes, there's different

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.340
file path dependencies.

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:12.110
Just because you
run a test locally

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:14.232
doesn't really give
you a ton of confidence

00:15:14.232 --> 00:15:16.190
that it's going to run
either in your CI system

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:17.961
or in production.

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:23.820
So why are these some of
the patterns that we see?

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:26.490
And we're not trying to present
these as anti-patterns, right?

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:27.690
Like, if something
goes down, you

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:29.773
should write a test to
cover it for the next time.

00:15:29.773 --> 00:15:31.470
You should do quality gates.

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:33.000
You should be testing locally.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.950
But I think what we
found is that, when

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:37.350
it gets to things like
integration testing,

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:39.660
it's just not that easy.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:40.780
It's not that easy to do.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:42.135
There's a lot of friction.

00:15:42.135 --> 00:15:43.830
Two of some of the
primary reasons,

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:45.690
I think, just as a
developer myself,

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.345
is that, the last thing you
want to do as a developer is

00:15:48.345 --> 00:15:51.030
to lose flow, is to
get out of the context

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:52.800
and have to wait
for a long build.

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.410
And if you are using
a hosted CI service

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:59.185
that you pay per concurrency,
or if you're self-hosting

00:15:59.185 --> 00:16:01.560
your infrastructure and you're
responsible for scaling it

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:05.950
up and down, it can lead to long
build times, long queue times.

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:09.540
So as a developer, you
find your own workarounds.

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:10.950
You test things locally.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:12.060
You write fewer tests.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:14.220
You test later on
in the process.

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:15.720
So this is where
some of the tooling

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:19.320
can really empower developers
to lower the bar of entry

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:22.742
for testing things
earlier on in the process.

00:16:22.742 --> 00:16:25.650
And the other aspect is
just environment management.

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:28.260
So it's simply just
somewhat difficult, often,

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:31.380
in different situations, to
provision infrastructure,

00:16:31.380 --> 00:16:34.470
manage that infrastructure,
spin up databases and spin them

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:37.080
down, things like that.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:40.127
So which leads us
to Cloud Build.

00:16:40.127 --> 00:16:42.210
So Cloud Build, if you've
been to the other talks,

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:42.810
you may have heard of.

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.162
If you're a GCB customer,
you may be using currently.

00:16:45.162 --> 00:16:46.620
But if you're not
familiar with it,

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:49.440
Cloud Build is a
hosted CI/CD service

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:53.998
on GCP for building, testing,
and deploying applications.

00:16:53.998 --> 00:16:55.040
It's generally available.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:57.330
It launched about two
years ago, originally

00:16:57.330 --> 00:16:58.770
called Container Builder.

00:16:58.770 --> 00:17:01.380
Last year at Next, we
announced it as Cloud Build,

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:05.208
as it covered a broader
spectrum of CI/CD use cases.

00:17:05.208 --> 00:17:06.750
And I think one of
the biggest things

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:08.417
that we hear people
really like about it

00:17:08.417 --> 00:17:11.609
is that it's serverless,
for whatever that word may

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:12.900
mean to you.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:14.609
It's a fully managed
infrastructure.

00:17:14.609 --> 00:17:17.069
And you pay for what you use,
so you pay per build minute.

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:19.140
So if you have a huge
CI infrastructure that's

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:20.849
idle most of the
time, you don't really

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:22.980
have to worry about that.

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:24.473
And the last thing--

00:17:24.473 --> 00:17:25.890
one of the
announcements this week

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:27.839
is, Cloud Build
is adding support

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:30.520
for hybrid and
multi-cloud workloads.

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:31.800
So it's currently in Alpha.

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:34.050
It's a new feature
called, Custom Workers.

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:36.390
But it's going to let you
use Cloud Build with a VPC

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:39.370
to connect to your
on-prem private networks.

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:41.250
So if you have a
self-hosted repository

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:45.480
or you're deploying to a
runtime like GKE on-prem,

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:47.580
Cloud Build is going
to be able to work

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:50.980
with managed infrastructure
with services and resources

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:51.855
behind your firewall.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:56.700
So let's take a
look real quick--

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:58.590
so how Cloud Build
works, there is

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:02.560
a build config file
that's in YAML, of course.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:06.390
And in that, you define what you
want Cloud Build to do for you.

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:08.005
So you define a
series of build steps.

00:18:08.005 --> 00:18:09.630
And in that config,
you could do things

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:13.410
like define your dependency
graph, environment variables.

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:14.910
It integrates with
KMS, so you can

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:18.002
do secret management,
as well as the artifacts

00:18:18.002 --> 00:18:18.960
that you want to build.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:24.420
So what this looks like
in your pipeline is,

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:28.050
you have often source
code in your repository.

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:29.700
You can set up
automated build triggers

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:32.430
so that when you commit to your
repo based on branch, or tag,

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:35.280
or pull request, it will
kick off a Cloud Build.

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.920
And in that build, you'll
run a series of build steps.

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:40.740
And so build steps are like
Jenkins stages, or GitHub

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:43.350
actions, or any
sort of CI systems

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:45.465
concept of jobs or tasks.

00:18:45.465 --> 00:18:48.930
And each build step is a
container in the Cloud Build

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:49.470
world.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.012
So anything that
can be containerized

00:18:51.012 --> 00:18:53.105
can be run as a build step.

00:18:53.105 --> 00:18:55.230
And at the end of your
build, there's some outputs.

00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:57.872
There's a status,
usually pass/fail,

00:18:57.872 --> 00:18:59.580
any artifacts that
were created, and then

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:01.663
any deployments that were
made, if you're using it

00:19:01.663 --> 00:19:04.610
for continuous delivery.

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:06.250
So what are some
common build steps?

00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:08.860
We have a whole bunch of
supported builder images

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:12.220
out-of-the-box, things like
Docker Build, Maven Build.

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:13.270
There's a GCloud one.

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:16.120
So any command with the Cloud
SDK you can run locally,

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:19.990
you can run in a hosted cloud
environment with Cloud Build.

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:23.290
And the last one is you could
bring your own container.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:24.730
So as I mentioned,
we have a bunch

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:27.310
of predefined ones for
the most popular tooling.

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:29.950
But if you have custom
tooling, custom versions

00:19:29.950 --> 00:19:32.860
you want to use, any
sort of arbitrary scripts

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:35.530
you want to run, if
you could containerize

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:37.900
that, then Cloud Build
can execute it for you

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:41.560
in a hosted environment.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:44.550
So if you start to think
about the possibilities there,

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:47.460
what that means is people
are often like, well,

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:49.470
is Cloud Build just
for building things?

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:53.453
And answer's, well, sort of
yes, but it could do a lot more.

00:19:53.453 --> 00:19:54.870
It could be used
for anything that

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:56.424
can run inside of a container.

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:00.980
So in the context of
integration testing--

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:02.980
which Dave is going to
show us a little bit more

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:05.345
tactically how this
works in practice--

00:20:05.345 --> 00:20:06.970
you could use some
of the common tools.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:08.530
So if you're using
Docker Compose,

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:10.180
you can run a
Docker Compose step,

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:11.680
spin up services
in the background,

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:13.810
like databases, run
tests against that,

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:16.450
all during your CI process.

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:19.470
It has support for things
like terraform and kubectl.

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:21.470
And then you can run
arbitrary scripts, as well.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:22.887
So if you have
existing tests that

00:20:22.887 --> 00:20:26.410
are shell scripts or Python
scripts that you want to run,

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:28.700
you can just simply wrap
those up in a Docker file

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:30.040
and run that, as well.

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:35.010
So just to wrap up
before I hand it off--

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:36.267
so why Cloud Build?

00:20:36.267 --> 00:20:38.100
I think first and
foremost, it's serverless.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:40.680
It handles a lot of the
boring stuff for you.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:41.970
It scales up and down.

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:43.560
You pay for what you use.

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:44.520
And it's isolated.

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:47.340
So every build step runs
inside of a container,

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:49.590
and every build
runs inside of a VM.

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:53.020
So a VM is your isolation
boundary, and it's ephemeral.

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:56.357
So we have workers ready
to go to run your jobs.

00:20:56.357 --> 00:20:58.440
And at the end of a build,
we'll spin down the VM.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:00.900
So anything that you may have
spun up during the time sort of

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:01.470
goes away.

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:03.480
You don't really have
to worry about managing

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:07.140
state through persistent
environments, things like that.

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:08.730
And the last is the flexibility.

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:12.120
So we give you a lot of the
out-of-the-box tools that are

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:13.260
the most common use cases.

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:15.270
But with things like
tests, a lot of teams

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:18.330
have their own processes,
have their own tooling

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:19.643
that they wrote around this.

00:21:19.643 --> 00:21:21.060
And really, Cloud
Build allows you

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:24.767
to run anything that, more
or less, you could run.

00:21:24.767 --> 00:21:26.850
And so with that I'm going
to hand it back to Dave

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:30.780
to show us this in
action a little bit more.

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:32.280
DAVE STANKE: Thank
you, Christopher.

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:33.863
All right, so let's
take a look at how

00:21:33.863 --> 00:21:35.940
we can use Cloud
Build to actually do

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:39.300
some of this cool
integration testing stuff.

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:42.155
So I'm going to show you a
demo that includes this sample

00:21:42.155 --> 00:21:42.655
application.

00:21:42.655 --> 00:21:46.290
This is a simplistic version
of a microservice application--

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:47.423
just two tiers.

00:21:47.423 --> 00:21:48.840
We've got a web
app that's running

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:52.243
Node.js and a DB that's MySQL.

00:21:52.243 --> 00:21:54.660
But of course, as engineers,
we know that the only numbers

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:56.730
are 0, 1, and
infinity, which means

00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:59.010
this has an infinite
number of microservices.

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:01.770
You can definitely scale
up these techniques.

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:05.430
And this just renders a
cloud cookie shop app.

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:07.740
And it runs inside Kubernetes.

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:09.750
You can use these
techniques for any kind

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:11.010
of integration testing.

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:13.050
But here, we're focused
on a microservice app

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:15.120
with the deployed
target Kubernetes.

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:16.620
Integration test
flow-- first, we're

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:18.570
going to build our containers.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:20.250
We have the web and the db.

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:22.470
Then we're going to
provision a test environment,

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:24.940
deploy the web and the
db containers to it.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:27.840
So now, we have a
system under test there.

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:30.790
And we're going to
run a test against it.

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:32.460
And so again, this
isn't an E2E test.

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:34.380
this is just a
point-in-time test,

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:38.440
but it does exercise both of
the layers of the application.

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:40.350
It's just going to curl
the endpoint and grep

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:42.460
for a particular string.

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:44.460
This is a cookie shop,
so what it's grepping for

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:46.830
is the words chocolate chip.

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:49.830
That's a success,
you know, clearly.

00:22:49.830 --> 00:22:52.050
If it passes, then we're good.

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:54.940
We don't need that
environment anymore,

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:56.130
and we'll deprovision it.

00:22:56.130 --> 00:22:59.020
And if it doesn't pass, the test
is going to end and fail there.

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:00.780
So most the time then,
the environment's

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:02.840
going to stay around
so we can debug it,

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:03.750
though there's some
trade-offs there

00:23:03.750 --> 00:23:04.990
that I'm going to show you.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:07.087
Now, in my experience,
the hard part of all this

00:23:07.087 --> 00:23:07.920
is the provisioning.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:08.910
There are a lot
of different ways

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:11.190
to do it, a lot of trade-offs
among the different ways

00:23:11.190 --> 00:23:11.852
to do it.

00:23:11.852 --> 00:23:13.810
So we're going to look
at different mechanisms,

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:15.870
different techniques
for provisioning

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:18.730
and deprovisioning
a system under test.

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:21.640
So the first one we're going
to look at is Docker Compose.

00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:24.930
Now, Docker Compose is basically
a wrapper around Docker.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.570
If you've ever run a Docker
Container with a dash d,

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:30.450
you're creating a service
that sticks around.

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:33.150
Docker Compose will spin
up multiple of those

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:37.110
and let them network
in between each other.

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:39.450
And in Cloud Build,
the way this happens

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:42.270
is that when you run Docker
Compose within Cloud Build--

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:45.180
and there's a supported
builder for Docker Compose that

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:49.570
lets you clone that and
run that into your project.

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:52.260
And so the way that works is
that the builder instantiates

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:53.880
these containers
that you're creating,

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:57.320
this composed network,
as sister containers.

00:23:57.320 --> 00:23:59.910
They share the same network
as the build steps of your

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:02.730
build so you could
interconnect between them.

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:06.360
And it's exposed on a Docker
network called the Cloud Build

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:11.270
network, and then other
steps can talk to them.

00:24:11.270 --> 00:24:12.310
So here's the flow.

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:13.870
We start from our
checkout from Git.

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:16.463
The gray box here represents
the boundary of Cloud Build.

00:24:16.463 --> 00:24:18.380
That's that security
boundary that Christopher

00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:21.580
was talking about where nothing
can get in or out without you

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:22.810
expressly doing it.

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:25.170
And it's actually out only.

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:27.170
And so the steps here
are, we're going to build,

00:24:27.170 --> 00:24:28.252
run some unit tests.

00:24:28.252 --> 00:24:30.460
And then we use Docker
Compose to create those sister

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:31.247
containers.

00:24:31.247 --> 00:24:32.830
And then we have our
integration test,

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:36.520
which is just a shell script,
asking whether it succeeded

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:37.640
or not.

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:40.210
And our build is over.

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:44.170
So the pros of this is
that it's fully ephemeral.

00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:46.750
Everything took place inside
that Cloud Build environment.

00:24:46.750 --> 00:24:50.260
And when the test is over,
everything gets torn down.

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:52.450
You don't pay for anything
after the test is over,

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:55.117
so there's no chance that you're
going to orphan those resources

00:24:55.117 --> 00:24:56.832
and be paying for them
after a long time.

00:24:56.832 --> 00:24:58.540
And for that reason,
it's cost-effective.

00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:02.600
You're only paying for
that Cloud Build run,

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:03.850
which is billed by the minute.

00:25:03.850 --> 00:25:06.680
And there's also a
generous free tier,

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:08.770
so you can get a lot
of mileage out of this.

00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:11.950
Now the cons are,
it's always ephemeral.

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:14.380
You can't get back in
afterwards and go poke around

00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:16.750
inside the Docker
Compose environment.

00:25:16.750 --> 00:25:20.640
I've done things where I write
a bunch of logs somewhere,

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:21.880
and it kind of works.

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:24.045
But you're still a
lot of spelunking

00:25:24.045 --> 00:25:26.170
to go and figure out, well,
what happened in there?

00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:28.360
So that's not necessarily ideal.

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:30.220
Limited fidelity--
because we're not

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:33.430
going to use Docker Compose as
our actual production runtime.

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:36.580
It's generally not going to
be your best choice for that.

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:39.550
And so therefore, it's not
exactly the same behaviors.

00:25:39.550 --> 00:25:41.140
You don't know if
what works here

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:42.730
is really going to work on prod.

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:44.980
There are some funny
things about startup order.

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:47.890
With Docker Compose, when
you start up the containers,

00:25:47.890 --> 00:25:49.030
they all just start.

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:51.700
And if they fail to
start, they all just fail.

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:54.010
And so when I was
working on this,

00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:57.590
I had the web app would
start up faster than the DB.

00:25:57.590 --> 00:25:59.770
The web app couldn't find
the DB, and it failed.

00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:02.020
I had to do some kind
of funky workaround

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:04.480
to make it so that
it would hang around

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:05.810
until the database came up.

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:07.810
And in fact, Docker Compose
used to have support

00:26:07.810 --> 00:26:10.000
for health checks and
removed those deliberately,

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:13.532
because it's not designed
to be a production runtime.

00:26:13.532 --> 00:26:15.490
And finally, the one that
I like the least here

00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:17.290
is the multiple configs.

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:19.695
You can't use the same
config for a Docker Compose

00:26:19.695 --> 00:26:21.570
environment as for your
production Kubernetes

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:22.410
environment.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:26.660
And inevitably, you're going
to have drift between those.

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:28.090
So let's try again--

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:29.390
method 2.

00:26:29.390 --> 00:26:32.530
So in this case, we're going
to use a GKE staging cluster

00:26:32.530 --> 00:26:36.952
and deploy all of our
tests to that same cluster.

00:26:36.952 --> 00:26:39.410
So that sounds like a lot of
things stomping on each other,

00:26:39.410 --> 00:26:41.290
but we're going to
isolate via namespaces.

00:26:41.290 --> 00:26:46.000
And if you're not familiar,
a namespace in Kubernetes,

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:47.590
it's an isolation
mechanism where

00:26:47.590 --> 00:26:51.620
you scope a number of resources
to a defined namespace.

00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:53.518
Now, namespaces can
talk to each other,

00:26:53.518 --> 00:26:55.060
but you have to do
that deliberately.

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:56.740
So by default,
everything in a namespace

00:26:56.740 --> 00:26:58.780
thinks that it's
in its own bubble.

00:26:58.780 --> 00:27:02.490
And unless you work
hard to undo this,

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:06.070
they're going to be
isolated together.

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:07.672
In Cloud Build,
the way we're going

00:27:07.672 --> 00:27:09.130
to do that is, on
each build, we're

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:12.650
going to create a uniquely named
namespace, deploy those things,

00:27:12.650 --> 00:27:13.150
test it.

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:15.760
And then on success, we'll
destroy that namespace.

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:18.135
Nice thing about namespace,
if you destroy the namespace,

00:27:18.135 --> 00:27:19.918
everything in it wipes
away along with it.

00:27:19.918 --> 00:27:21.460
So in this case,
what you'll see here

00:27:21.460 --> 00:27:23.300
is that we do our build
on our unit tests.

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:25.720
And then we're going to
use kubectl to deploy

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:30.130
a namespace into our
standing staging cluster.

00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:33.940
And you see, that's outside
of the Cloud Build network

00:27:33.940 --> 00:27:37.100
and, therefore, has a different
level of ephemerality.

00:27:37.100 --> 00:27:38.230
Then we run our test.

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:39.960
If we get to the
end, kubectl's going

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:42.820
to destroy that namespace
and all the resources in it.

00:27:42.820 --> 00:27:45.680
If we don't get to the end, that
last step isn't going to run,

00:27:45.680 --> 00:27:48.190
which means that namespace
is going to hang around

00:27:48.190 --> 00:27:51.460
so we can use any tools that we
can operate against the cluster

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:53.440
on to go look into
those containers,

00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:56.390
look at their disks, et cetera.

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:58.390
So the pros of this--
excellent fidelity,

00:27:58.390 --> 00:28:02.350
especially if your prod
happens to be running on GKE.

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:05.170
And I hope you've heard
some things this week that

00:28:05.170 --> 00:28:08.140
have helped you think that
maybe that's a good idea.

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:10.090
Easy tear-down--
delete the namespace,

00:28:10.090 --> 00:28:11.590
and everything's gone.

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:15.600
And we have that ephemerality
that we're looking for that,

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:16.810
on success, it disappears.

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:19.900
On failure, it hangs
around to go debug.

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:21.890
Cons-- imperfect installation.

00:28:21.890 --> 00:28:26.890
Now, it's unlikely, but it's
possible that one of your tests

00:28:26.890 --> 00:28:29.380
has runaway disk usage
or runaway memory.

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:31.630
Messes up another test that's
running at the same time

00:28:31.630 --> 00:28:32.830
on the same cluster--

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:33.700
unlikely.

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:35.830
But something that is more
likely to be a challenge

00:28:35.830 --> 00:28:38.920
is that you can't test
a cluster-wide resource.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:40.900
So for example,
the Agones project,

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:43.390
which has presentations
this week,

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:45.370
is the cool open
source framework

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:48.280
for running multi-player
games on Kubernetes.

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:51.130
The way it works is it creates
custom resource definitions.

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:53.860
And custom resource
definitions are cluster-wide.

00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:58.240
So they can't test
different versions of a CRD

00:28:58.240 --> 00:29:00.132
all in one cluster.

00:29:00.132 --> 00:29:01.840
Also, if you wanted
to validate something

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:04.382
against different versions of
Kubernetes, for example-- well,

00:29:04.382 --> 00:29:07.510
you can't have one cluster
that has multiple versions.

00:29:07.510 --> 00:29:10.260
The other problem is that
you may have orphaned forces

00:29:10.260 --> 00:29:13.910
that, if you fail a test and
you don't bother going in

00:29:13.910 --> 00:29:16.440
to look at that namespace
that didn't get deleted,

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:17.850
it's just going to hang around.

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:19.800
Not a huge impact.

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:22.453
Having a namespace with no
activity isn't a major problem.

00:29:22.453 --> 00:29:24.870
But you probably would want
to have some sort of a cleanup

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:27.390
script that runs once
a week or something

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:30.660
and just deletes everything
that everyone forgot about.

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:34.770
So now, if we wanted to
get absolute isolation,

00:29:34.770 --> 00:29:39.833
we can try using a Kubernetes
cluster for each test.

00:29:39.833 --> 00:29:41.250
And what we're
going to do here is

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:44.400
we're going create a
Kubernetes cluster in a VM

00:29:44.400 --> 00:29:46.080
so that each test has
its own completely

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:50.580
isolated environment, its own
VM that it's operating in.

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:53.165
And to do that, we're
going to provision

00:29:53.165 --> 00:29:54.540
that VM with
Terraform, and we're

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:57.993
going to install a single-node
Kubernetes application.

00:29:57.993 --> 00:29:59.160
There are several of these--

00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:01.780
MicroK8s, Minikube.

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:03.420
There's one called K3S.

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:06.960
These are all compliant
Kubernetes servers

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:09.480
that run on a single node.

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:12.390
And in order to offset some of
the ephemerality issues here,

00:30:12.390 --> 00:30:14.580
we're going to use a
self-destructing VM.

00:30:14.580 --> 00:30:18.142
So a self-destructing VM is one
that, if you forget about it,

00:30:18.142 --> 00:30:20.350
it won't just hang around
and cost you money forever.

00:30:20.350 --> 00:30:22.410
Instead, it's built in
with a timer mechanism.

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:24.785
And after a certain amount of
time, if no one touches it,

00:30:24.785 --> 00:30:25.917
it will delete itself.

00:30:25.917 --> 00:30:28.500
If you're interested in making
your own self-destructing VMs--

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:29.740
it's kind of fun--

00:30:29.740 --> 00:30:32.730
check out that bit.ly link.

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:35.500
So with this one, it's
similar to that GKE one,

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:38.250
except this VM is being
provisioned on-the-fly

00:30:38.250 --> 00:30:41.100
and has Kubernetes or a
Kubernetes-compliant server

00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:41.880
in it.

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:45.110
We're going to deploy our
application to that Kubernetes,

00:30:45.110 --> 00:30:46.110
test it.

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:49.300
And at the end, we're going to
use Terraform to tear it down.

00:30:49.300 --> 00:30:51.300
And again, if it doesn't
tear down automatically

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:53.370
because of a
success, it instead--

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:55.890
you can go and play
with it for a while.

00:30:55.890 --> 00:30:57.990
And it has an expiration date.

00:30:57.990 --> 00:31:00.930
It could be two hours, two days,
whatever-- it'll delete itself.

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:06.520
So what's good about this one?

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:10.230
Well, perfect isolation,
near-perfect fidelity.

00:31:10.230 --> 00:31:13.440
Again, these
non-Kubernetes Kubernetes,

00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:15.630
they are actually
certified compliant

00:31:15.630 --> 00:31:18.210
as being Kubernetes runtimes.

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:19.120
But who knows?

00:31:19.120 --> 00:31:21.640
Maybe they're not
exactly the same thing.

00:31:21.640 --> 00:31:24.180
You can test those
cluster-level resources.

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:26.640
And we've got that
ephemerality that we wanted.

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:29.970
Downside, it's still
not exactly GKE.

00:31:29.970 --> 00:31:31.410
And also, there's
the possibility

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:34.050
that these VMs hang around
longer than you want,

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:36.900
even if they have an expiration.

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:40.327
All right, finally, when I was
working on this stuff, people

00:31:40.327 --> 00:31:41.910
like Christopher
kept asking me, well,

00:31:41.910 --> 00:31:44.730
why don't you just create a
GKE cluster for every one?

00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:47.100
And I said, boy, that sounds
really expensive and slow.

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:48.330
But of course, it's possible.

00:31:48.330 --> 00:31:51.030
And by doing that, you of
course get all the fidelity,

00:31:51.030 --> 00:31:52.658
all the isolation.

00:31:52.658 --> 00:31:54.700
You can create the entire
production environment.

00:31:54.700 --> 00:31:55.530
You could even,
if you wanted to,

00:31:55.530 --> 00:31:58.160
clone in a database or
other things from production

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:00.870
so that every single
test has its own GKE.

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:02.010
And it can be done.

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:02.980
There's APIs for it.

00:32:02.980 --> 00:32:05.340
And I'm actually going to
show you as part of the test

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:07.800
how that works.

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:10.690
So let's check on that demo.

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:13.130
Let's see.

00:32:13.130 --> 00:32:14.030
Here we go.

00:32:14.030 --> 00:32:17.560
So what I did when I kicked off
that build back in my IDE is,

00:32:17.560 --> 00:32:20.200
I ran what I call a meta CI.

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:23.290
This is a Cloud Build invocation
that kicks off other Cloud

00:32:23.290 --> 00:32:24.250
Build invocations.

00:32:24.250 --> 00:32:27.227
And each one of those is one
of these testing mechanisms.

00:32:27.227 --> 00:32:29.560
In every case, we're testing
the exact same application,

00:32:29.560 --> 00:32:31.690
running the exact same
test script on it.

00:32:31.690 --> 00:32:34.480
But we're instantiating
and tearing down our test

00:32:34.480 --> 00:32:36.560
environment in different ways.

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:39.075
So we have all four of the
methods I just showed you.

00:32:39.075 --> 00:32:40.450
And if we look
into the UI, we'll

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:41.650
see that they all completed.

00:32:41.650 --> 00:32:44.590
And they all returned
the same result, but very

00:32:44.590 --> 00:32:46.470
different amounts of time.

00:32:46.470 --> 00:32:48.710
The Docker Compose
was very quick,

00:32:48.710 --> 00:32:51.500
because it didn't have to reach
to any external resources.

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:53.830
But GKE cluster per test?

00:32:53.830 --> 00:32:56.280
Pretty slow, but
perfect fidelity.

00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:58.163
And the other ones are
somewhere in between.

00:32:58.163 --> 00:32:59.830
So what's the right
choice for something

00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:00.580
that you're going to do?

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:02.205
It really depends on
what you're doing.

00:33:02.205 --> 00:33:04.330
Can we switch back to
the slides, please?

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:06.850
So in summary, we took
a look at a number

00:33:06.850 --> 00:33:09.340
of different mechanisms
for spinning up and testing

00:33:09.340 --> 00:33:12.020
against a microservice
production environment.

00:33:12.020 --> 00:33:15.130
And you can see they
all have pros and cons.

00:33:15.130 --> 00:33:18.070
For example, with Docker
Compose, it scales really big.

00:33:18.070 --> 00:33:21.040
You don't have any
extra resources.

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:23.798
That shared staging Kubernetes
is pretty good all around,

00:33:23.798 --> 00:33:25.840
but you don't necessarily
have perfect isolation.

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:27.663
A Kubernetes per
test should scale.

00:33:27.663 --> 00:33:29.080
But at some point,
you're probably

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:31.210
going to hit some quota
limits on IPs and things.

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:34.210
I haven't tried running tens
of thousands of those, though.

00:33:34.210 --> 00:33:36.450
It probably would
be kind of fun.

00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:38.325
All in all, when I was
working on this, folks

00:33:38.325 --> 00:33:39.880
were asking me, well,
what's the right one?

00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:41.172
And I said, oh, it all depends.

00:33:41.172 --> 00:33:42.940
It doesn't matter
what you're doing.

00:33:42.940 --> 00:33:45.760
But really on balance
for most use cases,

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:48.400
I think this is a really good
choice, this shared Kubernetes

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:49.300
environment.

00:33:49.300 --> 00:33:50.710
If you turn on
auto-scaling, it's

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:52.952
going to scale up and
scale down to meet

00:33:52.952 --> 00:33:54.160
the needs of your developers.

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:56.080
They're not going to
have standing resources

00:33:56.080 --> 00:33:59.920
beyond a minimum number
of nodes over the weekend.

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:02.650
You've got all of
that great fidelity.

00:34:02.650 --> 00:34:03.580
It's fast.

00:34:03.580 --> 00:34:06.980
And the isolation is probably
good enough for most workloads.

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:08.710
If it's not quite
there, maybe you

00:34:08.710 --> 00:34:11.739
add one of those more intense
things as a final check.

00:34:14.440 --> 00:34:15.478
We can do more.

00:34:15.478 --> 00:34:17.020
We can use these
same kind of methods

00:34:17.020 --> 00:34:20.050
to do deeper end-to-end
testing-- a scripted test that

00:34:20.050 --> 00:34:23.020
creates an order, puts
something in a shopping cart,

00:34:23.020 --> 00:34:24.460
buys some cookies.

00:34:24.460 --> 00:34:26.230
We could do mobile web testing.

00:34:26.230 --> 00:34:29.139
There's a service, called
Firebase Test Labs,

00:34:29.139 --> 00:34:31.630
that Google provides
to test against Android

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:34.120
devices and iOS.

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:35.830
And we could use
external resources.

00:34:35.830 --> 00:34:38.960
So say for example we wanted
to spin up a dedicated Cloud

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:42.400
SQL per test, we could do that.

00:34:42.400 --> 00:34:44.969
So this application and
all of these test methods

00:34:44.969 --> 00:34:45.969
are available on GitHub.

00:34:45.969 --> 00:34:49.239
I encourage you to
download it, give it a try.

00:34:49.239 --> 00:34:50.800
Pull requests are welcome.

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:52.659
Feedback is welcome.

00:34:52.659 --> 00:34:57.130
Speaking of feedback-- wait,
I'll get to that in a second.

00:34:57.130 --> 00:34:58.780
Speaking of feedback,
tweet at me.

00:34:58.780 --> 00:34:59.530
Here's my Twitter.

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:01.863
I'd love to hear from you,
what you're up to, how you're

00:35:01.863 --> 00:35:03.340
approaching problems like this.

00:35:03.340 --> 00:35:05.260
Christopher doesn't
really do Twitter.

00:35:05.260 --> 00:35:07.552
So if you want to get a
personalized response from him,

00:35:07.552 --> 00:35:09.910
just go to Cloud Build, and
go to that feedback link,

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:11.077
and he'll write back to you.

00:35:11.077 --> 00:35:13.030
It's like a stationary
calligraphy.

00:35:13.030 --> 00:35:14.350
It's great.

00:35:14.350 --> 00:35:14.980
I kid.

00:35:14.980 --> 00:35:17.200
But it is actually a
really responsive team.

00:35:17.200 --> 00:35:19.190
And they absolutely want
to hear your feedback.

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:20.620
And they're
absolutely listening.

